11
01:00:42,222 --> 01:00:48,222
The Da Vinci Code is a 2006 mystery thriller film directed by Ron Howard, written by
Akiva Goldsman, and based on Dan Brown's 2003 novel of the same name.

12
01:06:13,333 --> 01:06:19,333
The first in the Robert Langdon film series, the film stars Tom Hanks, Audrey Tautou, Ian
McKellen, Alfred Molina, Jürgen Prochnow, Jean Reno, and Paul Bettany.

13
01:11:44,444 --> 01:11:50,444
In the film, Robert Langdon, a professor of religious symbology from Harvard University, is the
prime suspect in the grisly and unusual murder of Louvre curator Jacques Saunière.

14
01:17:15,555 --> 01:17:21,555
On the body, the police find a disconcerting cipher and start an investigation.

15
01:22:46,666 --> 01:22:52,666
Langdon escapes with the assistance of police cryptologist Sophie
Neveu, and they begin a quest for the legendary Holy Grail.

16
01:28:17,777 --> 01:28:23,777
Sir Leigh Teabing, a noted British Grail historian, tells them that the actual Holy Grail
is explicitly encoded in Leonardo da Vinci's wall painting The Last Supper.

17
01:33:48,888 --> 01:33:54,888
Also searching for the Grail is a secret cabal within Opus Dei, an actual prelature of the Holy See,
who wish to keep the true Grail a secret to prevent the destruction of Christianity.

18
01:39:20,000 --> 01:39:26,000
The film, like the book, was considered controversial.

19
01:44:51,111 --> 01:44:57,111
It was met with especially harsh criticism by the Catholic Church for the accusation that it is behind a two-thousand-year-old cover-up concerning what the Holy Grail really is
and the concept that Jesus Christ and Mary Magdalene were married, producing a daughter, as well as its treatment of the organizations Priory of Sion and Opus Dei.

20
01:50:22,222 --> 01:50:28,222
Many members urged the laity to boycott the film.

21
01:55:53,333 --> 01:55:59,333
In the book, Brown states that the Priory of Sion and "all descriptions of artwork,
architecture, documents and secret rituals in this novel are accurate".

22
02:01:24,444 --> 02:01:30,444
The Da Vinci Code premiered as the opening film of the 59th Cannes Film Festival on May
17, 2006, and was theatrically released in the United States on May 19.

23
02:06:55,555 --> 02:07:01,555
The film received generally negative reviews from critics, but was a commercial success, grossing $224 million in its worldwide opening weekend and an initial total of $760
million worldwide against a $125 million production budget, becoming the second-highest-grossing film of 2006, as well as Howard's highest-grossing film to date.

24
02:12:26,666 --> 02:12:32,666
It was followed by two sequels, both directed by Howard:
Angels & Demons (2009) and Inferno (2016).

25
02:17:57,777 --> 02:18:03,777
The film explores themes of paris, france, based on novel
or book, holy grail, christianity, monk.

